  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1039 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe H500 hand analysis map at 23/00Z has a complex pattern\ndownstream with an compact Omega block over the Great Lakes\nregion on top of the larger SubTrop High still centered over the\nwestern Gulf states. Downstream of us there is a splitting\ntrough extending from the N.Rockies to SoCal/Baja. We are\nsqueezed between the trough and high which has allowing for a \ndeep fetch of subtropical moisture to invade our CWA over the\npast 24 hours with the KGJT RAOB PWAT gaining a third of an inch\nover the past 24 hours. This of course has fed the uptick in \nconvective coverage today with a mix of moderate/heavy rainfall\nstorms and gusty outflow winds over 50 mph. The interaction \nbetween the upstream trough and large high will play a role in \nthe heavy rain threat over a portion of our CWA over the next \nfew days. The northern split in the upstream trough looks to \nhave enough influence to keep a fetch of drier air flowing into \nour far western and northern CWA through tomorrow evening. While\nto the south moisture will be streaming in unabated East of the\n4 Corners into our southern and central mountains tonight \nthrough tomorrow morning. PWATs in this area look to be 150-200 \npercent of normal equating to the 99the percentile with a 10 \nyear return interval for early Fall. So the juice is there and \nwill be in the area through Thursday before flushing to the East\non Friday. Southerly flow into the terrain will create on \navenue of lift for squeezing this moisture out. A large \nanticyclonic jet in the faster flow aloft will be another which \nwill be joined by another speed max arriving Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. This secondary jet will be forced by a stronger \nwave/closed mid level system allowed to lift northward around \nthe SubTrop High by a weakening downstream trough. This forcing \nshould help provide stronger lift to help release instability \nand produce efficient rain processes early Thursday through the\nafternoon. This system will also help pull moisture back \nwestward and spread precipitation/cloud cover farther north and \nwest on Thursday. With moisture and lift in place there are high\nprobable chances of rainfall(and snow on some peaks?) across SW\nColorado tonight thru much of Thursday. The missing ingredient \nis instability to help drive heavier rainfall rates and \nthunderstorms. This should happen on the periphery of the cloud \ncover and deeper moisture today but under the rain shield to the\nSouth this isn't as clear. Sounding show moderate lapse rates \nand any CAPE in a long skinny profile. So some enhancement from \nlittle pieces energy and maybe orographics will help but the \nstronger storms remain on the edge where more sunshine is likely\nto occur. As the system lift through on Thursday there could be\nenough mixing to bring some peaks of sunshine and help produce \nsome stronger storms down South behind the lifting rain shield. \nFriday with the better moisture moving east and more possibility\nof sunshine should lead to more scattered thunderstorms. When \nall is said and done the most probable outcome for the heaviest \nprecipitation remains over the southern San Juans and adjacent \nvalleys and where PoPs will be the highest for this event. QPF \namounts have come down slightly from previous forecast but still\nwidespread 1-2 inches in this area have at least a 70 percent \nchance of probability. Therefore the Flood Watch in place looks \non track. So we will be watching for the threat of flash \nflooding in smaller basins and as in the last event some of the \nmain stem rivers in southern Colorado are likely to see a jump \nin stages as this precipitation runs off. We should see a drying\ntrend for the weekend with another push of moisture coming back\nlate Sunday to start the new week on the wetter side. \nTemperatures will be seasonable to cool depending on cloud cover\nand precipitation overhead the next few days with a warmer \nweekend on tap.",
